course: physics
lesson: 23
title: Heat moves: hot to cold
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Heat always flows from a hotter thing to a colder thing, never the other way, until both are the same temperature.
explain: That is why your hot cocoa cools on the table and an ice cube melts in your warm hand, and why your hand feels cold: it is losing its heat to the ice.
explain: Heat travels in three ways: along solid things, as in a metal spoon that gets hot at the handle; carried by moving air or water, as warm air rises from a radiator; and as invisible rays, as a campfire warms your face across the cold night air.
explain: Your skin does not measure temperature; it feels how fast heat leaves it, so a metal bench feels colder than a wooden one on the same frosty morning, though both are equally cold.
explain: A thermometer is the honest judge: its liquid grows longer as it warms and shrinks as it cools.
fact: Heat always flows from hotter things to colder things.
fact: Things cool or warm until they reach the same temperature as their surroundings.
fact: Metals pass heat along very well, so they are good conductors, while wood and plastic pass heat badly.
fact: A metal bench and a wooden bench at the same temperature feel different because metal draws heat out of your hand faster.
fact: Your hand feels cold when you hold ice because it is losing heat to the ice.
fact: A lid slows the cooling of a hot drink, because less steam escapes.
fact: Blowing on soup sweeps away the warm, steamy air above it, so more steam escapes and carries heat away.
fact: Warm air spreads out, becomes lighter for its size and rises, while cool air sinks to take its place, so a radiator warms a whole room.
fact: A fire and the Sun warm you with invisible heat rays that can cross air and even empty space.
fact: Melting needs heat, which is why ice cream on a summer table melts and a cool box with ice packs slows it down.
fact: The liquid in a thermometer grows longer as it warms; body temperature is about 37 degrees, and a fever is higher.
fact: A puddle dries because water slowly turns into invisible water vapour even below boiling, and wind makes it dry faster.
exercise: Which way does heat always flow? | From hotter things to colder things | From colder things to hotter things | Always upwards | Only from metal to wood
exercise: Why does the handle of a metal spoon in hot tea get hot? | Heat travels along the metal from the hot end | The hot steam climbs up the handle to your hand | Metal makes its own heat | The tea flows inside the spoon
exercise: Why does a wooden spoon in hot soup stay cool at the handle? | Wood passes heat along badly | Wood is always cold inside | Wood blows the heat away | Wood soaks up the soup instead
exercise: On a cold morning, why does a metal bench feel colder than a wooden one? | Metal draws heat out of your hand faster | The metal bench is really much colder than the air | Wood makes its own warmth | The wooden bench was in the sun
exercise: Why does your hand feel cold when you hold an ice cube? | Your hand is losing heat to the ice | The ice is giving cold to your hand | Your blood stops moving | The water freezes your skin
exercise: Why does hot cocoa cool down if you leave it? | Heat moves from the drink into the cooler air and cup | The cold in the room creeps into the drink | The cocoa uses up its heat by itself | The milk turns cold on its own
exercise: How does putting a lid on hot cocoa change its cooling? | It slows it down because less steam escapes | It speeds it up | It stops it completely, so the cocoa stays hot for ever | It makes the drink hotter
exercise: Why does blowing on hot soup cool it? | It sweeps away warm steamy air so more heat can leave | Your breath is full of ice | It pushes the heat back into the bowl | It makes the soup heavier
exercise: Why does warm air rise above a radiator? | Warm air spreads out and becomes lighter for its size | Radiators blow the warm air upwards with a small hidden fan | Cold air pushes it down | Warm air is pulled up by the ceiling
exercise: How does a campfire warm your face on a cold night? | With invisible heat rays that travel straight to you | By the smoke landing on your face | By the cold night air around you carrying the heat to you | Only by the light being bright
exercise: How does the Sun's heat reach the Earth across empty space? | As rays, like light | Carried by warm air | Along a chain of rocks | It does not reach us at all
exercise: Why does ice cream melt on a summer table? | The warm air and table give it heat | The ice cream makes its own heat inside | Sunlight turns it back into plain milk | The bowl sucks all the cold out of it
exercise: How does a thermometer show the temperature? | Its liquid grows longer when warm and shorter when cold | It changes colour at every degree | It counts how much you sweat | It measures how loud the air is
exercise: What is normal body temperature? | About 37 degrees | About 20 degrees | About 50 degrees | About 100 degrees
exercise: Why does a puddle disappear on a sunny day? | Its water slowly turns into invisible water vapour | It sinks into the ground and no more | The Sun drinks it up through a long hot beam of light | It boils in the sunshine
exercise: Why do puddles dry faster on a windy day? | The wind blows the water vapour away | The wind pushes the water into drains | The wind is always hot | The wind freezes the puddle away
test: My little bro says the ice gives "cold" to your hand. How would you put him right? | Your hand gives heat to the ice; cold is not a thing that moves | He's right, cold flows into your hand | Ice gives out cold rays | Hand and ice swap their colours
test: On a scorching day, which feels hotter to sit on: a metal bench or a wooden one? | The metal one, because it passes heat into you faster | The wooden one, because wood stores heat | Both feel exactly the same | Neither, sun does not heat benches
test: What would happen to a cup of hot tea left in a warm room for a very long time? | It would cool until it was the same temperature as the room | It would stay hot for ever | It would get colder than the room | It would get hotter by itself
test: Why do we put a radiator low down under a window rather than near the ceiling? | Warm air rises and sets the whole room's air circling | Heat only travels downwards | Radiators are too heavy for walls | It does not matter at all where in the room it is fixed up
test: In a cartoon, a character holds a mug of tea and steam curls into a heart. What is the steam doing for the tea? | Carrying heat away and cooling it | Keeping it warm by staying close to it | Making the tea stronger and darker | Slowly turning the tea into hot cocoa
test: shut up and pass me a metal spoon for the soup, it won't get hot. Is he right? | No, a metal spoon gets hot at the handle | Yes, metal never gets hot, however long it sits | Yes, only plastic spoons ever get hot in soup | No, but only spoons made of real silver get hot
test: You feel your forehead and think you have a fever. What is the honest judge? | A thermometer | How hot your hand feels | How hot your face looks in the mirror | How tired you feel
test: Why does the Sun warm you even though space between us is empty? | Heat travels as rays that need no air | Space is full of warm air all the way here | The Moon catches the heat and passes it on | Heat only comes up from the ground below
test: Which is true about how you feel hot and cold? | Your skin feels how fast heat leaves it, not the true temperature | Your skin is a perfect thermometer | Your skin only feels cold, never heat | Your skin feels the colour of things
test: Why does a picnic cool box with ice packs keep ice cream from melting quickly? | It keeps heat from reaching the ice cream | It makes the ice cream heavier and harder to melt | It turns heat into light | It stops the Sun from rising
exam: Identify the direction in which heat is invariably transferred between two bodies in contact. | From the body of higher temperature to that of lower temperature | From the body of lower temperature to that of higher temperature | From the larger body to the smaller | In no consistent direction
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sensation of coldness upon touching a metal object at ambient temperature? | Heat is withdrawn from the hand more rapidly by the metal | The metal is necessarily of lower temperature than its surroundings | The metal emits cold into the hand | The sensation is wholly imaginary
exam: It is given that the space between the Sun and the Earth is very nearly empty. Ascertain the mode by which solar heat reaches the Earth. | By radiation | By conduction through matter | By the circulation of air | By the motion of water
exam: State the principle whereby a liquid-in-glass thermometer indicates temperature. | The liquid expands upon warming and contracts upon cooling | The liquid alters its colour with temperature | The glass emits light in proportion to the heat | The liquid evaporates at a fixed rate
